{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# FUNCTIONS IN PYTHON"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "References:\n",
    "- resources on `return` statement: https://realpython.com/python-return-statement/\n",
    "- resources on python functions: https://realpython.com/defining-your-own-python-function/#argument-passing\n",
    "- Book: Python all-in-one for dummies"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Gazillion Lines, the Old-Fashioned Way"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You have been tasked with developing a very important calculator function that adds two numbers and prints out the sum. How would we go about it in the old-fashioned way?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# We need to develop a program that adds two numbers, the old-fashioned way\n",
    "num_1 = 2\n",
    "num_2 = 3\n",
    "sum = num_1 + num_2\n",
    "# we expect the sum to be 5, or is it?\n",
    "print(sum)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We have provided 4 lines of code. What if we want to repeat the calculation three times?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5\n",
      "7\n",
      "9\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# round 1\n",
    "num_1 = 2\n",
    "num_2 = 3\n",
    "sum = num_1 + num_2\n",
    "print(sum)\n",
    "\n",
    "#round 2\n",
    "num_1 = 4\n",
    "num_2 = 3\n",
    "sum = num_1 + num_2\n",
    "print(sum)\n",
    "\n",
    "#round 3\n",
    "num_1 = 4\n",
    "num_2 = 5\n",
    "sum = num_1 + num_2\n",
    "print(sum)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Here, we have 12 lines of code to execute the summation of three pairs of numbers. Imagine now if we need to develop a more elaborate NASA-level calculator that has multiplication, division, addition and subtraction. We would be dealing with maybe a gazillion lines of code, as we will need a new set of code for every new execution of the mathematical functions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Huh! You did not see it coming, did you? Functions. Functions in mathematics are a set of \"rules\" that allow for solving of problems within a sub-domain. An example is the equation of a straight line, y = mx+c. This function applies to all straight lines. You do not have to derive the properties of a straight line from first principles every time you encounter one.\n",
    "\n",
    "What if we transfer the concept of functions from mathematics to our Sum program?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Functions, the New Way"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 71,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5\n",
      "7\n",
      "9\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# defining a function\n",
    "def sum_num(x,y): #providing parameters\n",
    "    '''\n",
    "    creating a 'template' that can be applied to any two sets of numbers that require summing.\n",
    "    '''\n",
    "    sum = x + y\n",
    "    print(sum)\n",
    "\n",
    "# performing calculations to three sets of numbers\n",
    "(sum_num(2,3)) #passing arguments\n",
    "(sum_num(3,4)) #passing arguments\n",
    "(sum_num(4,5)) #passing arguments"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We have the same output as the old-fashioned code, but with much less lines of code: 6 (50% less code)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Functions in the World\n",
    "- Mathematical formulae\n",
    "- Living organism's DNA\n",
    "- "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "So,\n",
    "\n",
    "## What are functions in programming?\n",
    "\n",
    "- named code block that performs some actions with the purpose of computing a final value or result, which is then sent back to the caller code.\n",
    "\n",
    "It is important to differentiate it from a **procedure**, which is a named code block that performs a set of actions without computing a final value or result.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why do we write functions?\n",
    "- Allows for easier implementation of a program map, as functions allow us to conceive our program as a bunch of sub-steps\n",
    "- allow for re-use of code\n",
    "- allows for re-use of variable names, therefore manageable code namespace\n",
    "- allows for isolated testing of sections of our program\n",
    "- allows for change or fixing of code in only one location\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## What would the world be without functions?\n",
    "\n",
    "* "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Steps to writing a function:\n",
    "- Understand the purpose of the function\n",
    "- Define the data that comes into the function from the caller (in the form of parameters)!\n",
    "- Define what data variables are needed inside the function to accomplish its goal.\n",
    "- Decide on the set of steps that the program will use to accomplish this goal. (The Algorithm)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Getting Our Hands Dirty"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 1. Creating a Function\n",
    "\n",
    "#### *`def`ining a Function*\n",
    "\n",
    "To create a function, start a new line with `def` followed by a space, and then a name of your own choosing followed by a pair of parenthesis `()` and finally a colon `:` at the end.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "ename": "SyntaxError",
     "evalue": "incomplete input (2350046573.py, line 1)",
     "output_type": "error",
     "traceback": [
      "\u001b[1;36m  Cell \u001b[1;32mIn[4], line 1\u001b[1;36m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;33m    def hello():\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;37m                ^\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;31mSyntaxError\u001b[0m\u001b[1;31m:\u001b[0m incomplete input\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def hello():"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Running the code generates a syntax error, indicating that the translator is expecting more input than what is presented. We can, at minimum, add a `pass` keyword as follows:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def hello():\n",
    "    pass"
   ]
