  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Our code is now 5 lines. Note that we have passed the summing operation directly to the return keyword. This helps to reduce the lines of code. Only ensure that it is clear.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### *Explicit Return Statement*\n",
    "\n",
    "Functions basically can return any Python object to the caller code, using an explicit return statement:\n",
    "- numeric values `int, float, and complex` values, \n",
    "- collections and sequences of objects `list, tuple, dictionary, or set` objects, \n",
    "- binary values `True` or `False`\n",
    "- boolean operator `not`\n",
    "- comparison operators `==, !=, >, >=, <, <=`\n",
    "- membership operator `in`\n",
    "- identity operator `is`\n",
    "- user-defined objects, \n",
    "- classes, \n",
    "- functions,\n",
    "- modules or packages\n",
    "- ...\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that you can return more than one object, eg, `return sum, x, y`\n",
    "\n",
    "#### *Implicit Return Statement*\n",
    "\n",
    "An implicit return statement (return is omitted, or a bare return without a return value is used) returns the following:\n",
    "- `None`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
