  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Implicit return statements can be used to generate *side effects* such as:\n",
    "- printing something to the screen, expecially useful when scripting. An example is our first implementation of sum_num()\n",
    "- modifying a global variable (NOT RECOMMENDED)\n",
    "- updating the state of an object\n",
    "- writing some text to a file\n",
    "- ..."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# describing how to change a global variable\n",
    "counter = 0\n",
    "def increment(): # a function that changes a global variable\n",
    "    global counter\n",
    "    counter += 1\n",
    "\n",
    "increment()\n",
    "print(counter)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The example above shows a function that changes a global variable. The result of calling `increment()` will depend on the initial value of `counter`. Different initial values for `counter` will generate different results, so the function’s result can’t be controlled by the function itself.\n",
    "\n",
    "Modifying global variables is generally considered a bad programming practice. Just like programs with complex expressions, programs that modify global variables can be **difficult to debug, understand, and maintain**. The function uses the `global` statement, which is also considered a bad programming practice in Python.\n",
    "\n",
    "When you modify a global variable, you’re potentially affecting all the functions, classes, objects, and any other parts of your programs that rely on that global variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "To understand a program that modifies global variables, you need to be aware of all the parts of the program that can see, access, and change those variables. So, **good practice recommends writing self-contained functions that take some arguments and return a useful value (or values) without causing any side effect on global variables**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A remedy would be to write a self-contained `increment()` that takes arguments and returns a coherent value that **depends only on the input arguments**, as shown below:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 21,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "counter = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "def increment(var):\n",
    "    return var + 1\n",
    "\n",
    "print(increment (counter))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 24,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print (counter) ## value of counter is not changed outside of increment() \n",
    "                 # therefore does not lead to unexpected issues in the program"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "When there is need to change the value of `counter`, it will be done so explicitly with a call to `increment()`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 25,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "1\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# explicit increase to the value of counter by 1\n",
    "counter = increment(counter)\n",
    "print (counter)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This way, you’ll have more control over what’s happening with `counter` throughout your code."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### *Using returns with Conditions*"
