    "\n",
    "The `pass` keyword, also known as the **null operation**, has been used as a placement statement and does not peform any action but is to ensure that we do not encounter syntax errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "We can now add some line of code:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# defining a function\n",
    "def hello():\n",
    "    print ('Hello')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### *Commenting a Function*\n",
    "\n",
    "Many people will interact with the code of program within its lifecycle. Most will not be the original authors of the code, but they will be able to improve on the code to make a program more efficient and/or better, and this is due to optimum use of comments and docstrings. This is the same way scientific inventions have been able to be passed through several generations through writing, intentional writing.\n",
    "\n",
    "How do we comment python functions?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In general, a function takes **arguments** (in our case: x & y), **performs** some operations, and **returns** a value (or object). The value that a function returns to the caller is generally known as the function’s return value."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 75,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def hello():    # <short description of function's task eg. \"Sum of two numbers\">\n",
    "    ''' <Describe the function, giving a summary of the function's pseudocode, and\n",
    "        a description of the input variables, if any> '''"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 76,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Help on function hello in module __main__:\n",
      "\n",
      "hello()\n",
      "    <Describe the function, giving a summary of the function's pseudocode, and\n",
      "    a description of the input variables, if any>\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "help(hello)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2. Calling a Function\n",
    "\n",
    "Running the cell above yields no visible output even though we gave the instruction to print the word \"Hello\". This is okay and expected. It is not an error. We have done above is *Defining a Function*. Note that you should only define **once** a function.\n",
    "\n",
    "Its powers can be experienced once it is *called*. Calling a function is calling it to perform the tasks that it has been given. You can call a function as much as you need, and with that, you reduce the lines of code that you would otherwise need to type every time you need a certain functionality. \n",
    "\n",
    "And if there is some change you need to incorporate into that functionality, you need to adjust the function definition only, therefore very convenient."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# calling a function\n",
    "hello()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "After calling the function, you must have noticed that \"Hello\" has been printed out. Note that you do not include the keyword `def` and the colon when calling a function."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3. Function Arguments"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### *Positional Arguments/Parameters*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In the example function **definition** `def sum_num(x,y):`, the x & y are referred to as **positonal arguments** or **required arguments**. They are a comma-separated list within the function parenthesis. The arguments can also be referred to as **local variables**\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# function that computes sum of two numbers and outputs in the form x+y=sum\n",
    "def sum_num(x,y):\n",
    "    sum = x + y\n",
    "    return print(x,\"+\",y,\"=\",sum)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "When the function is **called**, a corresponding list of arguments MUST be provided.\n",
    "1. `sum_num()` will raise a TypeError as the instance is missing 2 required positional arguments, as shown below:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "ename": "TypeError",
