    "my_print(**my_dict) # equivalent to my_print(name=\"Alice\", age=25, city=\"New York\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Practical applications of `*` and `**` operators in functions:\n",
    "1. You can use *args and **kwargs to write decorators that can wrap any function, regardless of its arguments. Decorators are functions that modify the behavior of another function without changing its code."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import time\n",
    "\n",
    "def timer(func):\n",
    "    \"\"\"a decorator that prints the execution time of any function:\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        func (_type_): _description_\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n",
    "        start = time.time()\n",
    "        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "        end = time.time()\n",
    "        print(f\"{func.__name__} took {end - start} seconds to run.\")\n",
    "        return result\n",
    "    return wrapper\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "2. You can use *args and **kwargs to write a function that can forward requests to different handlers based on some condition."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def handle_request(method, *args, **kwargs):\n",
    "    \"\"\"a function that can handle different types of HTTP requests:\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        method (_type_): _description_\n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        _type_: _description_\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    if method == \"GET\":\n",
    "        return handle_get(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    elif method == \"POST\":\n",
    "        return handle_post(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    elif method == \"PUT\":\n",
    "        return handle_put(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    elif method == \"DELETE\":\n",
    "        return handle_delete(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        return \"Invalid method\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "3. You can use *args and **kwargs to write a function that can create objects of different classes based on some argument. For example, you can write a function that can create different types of animals:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def create_animal(animal_type, *args, **kwargs):\n",
    "    \"\"\"a function that can create different types of animals:\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        animal_type (str): _description_\n",
    "    Returns:\n",
    "        _type_: _description_\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    if animal_type == \"dog\":\n",
    "        return Dog(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    elif animal_type == \"cat\":\n",
    "        return Cat(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    elif animal_type == \"bird\":\n",
    "        return Bird(*args, **kwargs)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        return \"Unknown animal type\"\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4. `return`ing Function Value"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Function value is the final value of computations handled within a function.\n",
    "An important part of a function is the builtin `return` keyword. It enables the return of the function value to the caller code.\n",
    "\n",
    "We can rewrite `sum_num()` to incorporate the `return` keyword:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 64,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5\n",
      "7\n",
      "9\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# defining a function\n",
    "def sum_num(x,y): # function that computes the sum of two numbers\n",
    "    '''\n",
    "    creating a 'template' that can be applied to any two sets of numbers that require summing.\n",
    "    eg. sum of 1 + 2 = 3\n",
    "    return value is 3\n",
    "    '''\n",
    "    return (x + y)\n",
    "\n",
    "# performing calculations to three sets of numbers\n",
    "print(sum_num(2,3))\n",
    "print(sum_num(3,4))\n",
    "print(sum_num(4,5))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 66,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5 + 7 + 9 = 21\n",
      "21\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "sum_grand = sum_num(2,3) + sum_num(3,4) + sum_num(4,5)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(sum_num(2,3), \"+\", sum_num(3,4), \"+\", sum_num(4,5), \"=\", sum_grand)\n",
    "print(sum_grand)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Our code is now 5 lines. Note that we have passed the summing operation directly to the return keyword. This helps to reduce the lines of code. Only ensure that it is clear.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### *Explicit Return Statement*\n",
    "\n",
    "Functions basically can return any Python object to the caller code, using an explicit return statement:\n",
    "- numeric values `int, float, and complex` values, \n",
    "- collections and sequences of objects `list, tuple, dictionary, or set` objects, \n",
    "- binary values `True` or `False`\n",
    "- boolean operator `not`\n",
    "- comparison operators `==, !=, >, >=, <, <=`\n",
    "- membership operator `in`\n",
    "- identity operator `is`\n",
    "- user-defined objects, \n",
    "- classes, \n",
    "- functions,\n",
    "- modules or packages\n",
    "- ...\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that you can return more than one object, eg, `return sum, x, y`\n",
    "\n",
    "#### *Implicit Return Statement*\n",
    "\n",
    "An implicit return statement (return is omitted, or a bare return without a return value is used) returns the following:\n",
    "- `None`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "5\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# with a return statement\n",
    "def sum_num(x,y): # function that computes the sum of two numbers\n",
    "    sum = x + y\n",
    "    return sum\n",
    "    \n",
    "value = sum_num(2,3) # expect 5 as sum\n",
    "print (value)"
   ]
