   "source": [
    "#### *Decorator Functions*\n",
    "Definition: a function that takes another function as an argument and returns a modified version of it after adding or changing the behavior of the original function without permanently altering it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To create a decorator function in Python, you need to follow these steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Define an outer function that takes the original function as an argument. This is the **decorator function**.\n",
    "- Define an inner function that wraps the original function and adds some functionality to it. This is the **wrapper function**.\n",
    "- Return the inner function from the outer function.\n",
    "- Use the `@` symbol followed by the decorator function name to apply the decorator to the original function."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Examples of common built-in decorator functions include `@property`, `@classmethod` and `@staticmethod`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 25,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello!\n",
      "\n",
      "\n",
      "Before the function\n",
      "Hello!\n",
      "After the function\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Define the decorator function\n",
    "def my_decorator(func):\n",
    "    # Define the wrapper function\n",
    "    def wrapper(): # name wrapper is normally used due to its intruitivness\n",
    "        # Do something before the original function\n",
    "        print(\"Before the function\")\n",
    "        # Call the original function\n",
    "        func()\n",
    "        # Do something after the original function\n",
    "        print(\"After the function\")\n",
    "    # Return the wrapper function\n",
    "    return wrapper\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define the original function\n",
    "def say_hello():\n",
    "    print(\"Hello!\\n\\n\")\n",
    "    \n",
    "say_hello()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Apply the decorator to the original function\n",
    "@my_decorator\n",
    "def say_hello():\n",
    "    print(\"Hello!\")\n",
    "    \n",
    "say_hello()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "You might have noted that `@my_decorator` serves the same purpose as `my_decorator(say_hello)`. This is because `@my_decorator` taking the function that it immediately preceeds as its argument."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Notable uses of decorator functions include implementation of:\n",
    "- logging\n",
    "- input validation\n",
    "- caching\n",
    "- timing\n",
    "- authentication features"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### *`map()`, `filter()` and `reduce()` functions*\n",
    "\n",
    "`map()` applies a function to each element of an iterable and returns a map object.\n",
    "\n",
    "`filter()` selects the elements of an iterable that satisfy a condition and returns a filter object.\n",
    "\n",
    "`reduce()` applies a function to the elements of an iterable and returns a single value."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 26,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "6\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from functools import reduce\n",
    "\n",
    "numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n",
    "even = filter(lambda x : x % 2 == 0, numbers) # filter the even numbers\n",
    "sum = reduce(lambda x, y : x + y, even) # add the even numbers\n",
    "print(sum) # 6\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### *Generator Functions*\n",
    "\n",
    "Functions that can produce a sequence of values using the yield keyword."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Generators are useful when you need to create an iterable that is too large to fit in memory, or when you want to lazily evaluate the elements of an iterable. This means that they only compute the next value when it is requested, thus can save memory and improve performance when working with large or infinite data sets.\n",
    "\n",
    "Generators can also be used to create coroutines, which are functions that can pause and resume their execution, thus enabling asynchronous and concurrent programming.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Some of the benefits of generator functions in Python are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- They are easy to write and read, using the `yield` keyword instead of return to produce values.\n",
    "- They can be used with built-in functions and modules that work with iterables, such as for loops, map, filter, reduce, itertools, etc.\n",
    "- They can be composed together, creating pipelines of data processing.\n",
    "- They can be used to create custom iterators, without having to implement the iterator protocol (`iter` and `next` methods).\n",
    "- They can be used to create coroutines, using the `yield` `from` and `send` methods to communicate between them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For example, you can use a generator to create an infinite sequence of Fibonacci numbers:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "<generator object fibonacci at 0x000001B190D01D80>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 30,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def fibonacci():\n",
    "    a = 0\n",
    "    b = 1\n",
    "    while True:\n",
    "        yield a\n",
    "        a, b = b, a + b\n",
    "\n",
    "fibonacci()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "0\n",
      "a: 0\n",
      "1\n",
      "b: 1\n",
      "1\n",
      "c: 1\n",
      "2\n",
      "c: 2\n",
      "3\n",
      "c: 3\n",
      "5\n",
      "c: 5\n",
      "8\n",
      "c: 8\n",
      "13\n",
      "c: 13\n",
      "21\n",
      "c: 21\n",
      "34\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Define the generator function\n",
    "def fibonacci():\n",
    "    # Initialize the first two Fibonacci numbers\n",
    "    a = 0\n",
    "    b = 1\n",
    "    # Yield the first Fibonacci number\n",
    "    yield a\n",
    "    print(f'a: {a}')\n",
    "    # Yield the second Fibonacci number\n",
    "    yield b\n",
    "    print(f'b: {b}')\n",
    "    # Loop indefinitely\n",
    "    while True:\n",
    "        # Calculate the next Fibonacci number\n",
    "        c = a + b\n",
    "        # Yield the next Fibonacci number\n",
    "        yield c\n",
    "        print(f'c: {c}')\n",
    "        # Update the previous two Fibonacci numbers\n",
    "        a = b\n",
    "        b = c\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create a generator object\n",
    "fib = fibonacci()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Print the first 10 Fibonacci numbers\n",
    "for i in range(10):\n",
    "    print(next(fib))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def read_file(filename):\n",
    "    \"\"\"A generator function that reads a file line by line\n",
    "\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        filename (str): _description_\n",
    "\n",
    "    Yields:\n",
    "        str: _description_\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    with open(filename) as f:\n",
    "        for line in f:\n",
    "            yield line\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def filter_even(iterable):\n",
    "    \"\"\"A generator function that filters out even numbers from an iterable.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Args:\n",
    "        iterable (_type_): _description_\n",
    "\n",
    "    Yields:\n",
    "        _type_: _description_\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    for item in iterable:\n",
    "        if item % 2 == 0:\n",
    "            yield item\n"
   ]
